```
@id("read-only-lookups")
permit (
  principal,
  action in [
    Drupe::Action::"list_workorders",
    Drupe::Action::"get_approval_record",
    Drupe::Action::"get_workorder_tasks",
    Drupe::Action::"get_workorder_costs",
    Drupe::Action::"get_workorder_actuals_vs_planned",
    Drupe::Action::"get_workorder_kpis",
    Drupe::Action::"get_schedule_calendar",
    Drupe::Action::"get_my_assigned_workorders",
    Drupe::Action::"get_failure_codes"
  ],
  resource
);

// Must be permitted on its own, otherwise its completion is never recorded as a response.
@id("authority-approval-lookup")
permit (principal, action == Drupe::Action::"authority_get_approval", resource);

@id("close-after-human-signoff")
permit (principal, action == Drupe::Action::"close_workorder", resource)
when { context.input.site_id == "MAIN" }
when temporal {
  formerly within 1h Drupe::Action::"authority_get_approval"::response{
    eventResource: resource,
    input.wonum: context.input.wonum,
    input.site_id: context.input.site_id,
    output.decision: "SIGNED_OFF",
    output.approver_kind: "OPERATOR"
  }
};

@id("never-approve-directly")
forbid (principal, action == Drupe::Action::"approve_workorder", resource);
```

Closing is permitted only at MAIN, and only if `authority_get_approval` returned a response in this session within the last hour for the same `wonum` and `site_id`. That response must show `decision` "SIGNED_OFF" and `approver_kind` "OPERATOR". An attempt that was denied or failed is recorded as an error, so it never counts.

Two assumptions to check:
- **Approval source:** I used `authority_get_approval` as the source of the approval, because its description says it returns the decision of record and whether the approver is human. I did not accept `get_approval_record` as proof, although it stays available as a read-only lookup.
- **Value strings:** the `authority` server's output fields are plain strings with no declared values. I assumed it uses "SIGNED_OFF" and "OPERATOR", the same values `get_approval_record` declares. If the authority uses different strings, the close policy never matches and closing is always denied.

The agent has no permit for `approve_workorder`, and an explicit `forbid` backs that up. The other mutating tools (`generate_work_order`, `update_workorder`, `assign_technician`, `cancel_workorder`) have no permit either, so they are denied by default.
